WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.280
Speaker 1: It's a personal attack because they feel.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.240
Speaker 1: People who believe this feel like there's nothing wrong.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.960
Speaker 1: Every emotion you feel is to be expressed.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.440
Speaker 1: Every craving is to be satisfied.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.279
Speaker 1: And if you now tell the person is wrong, it hits to the core.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:17.680
Speaker 1: It just feels like they are not in control.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.960
Speaker 1: It reveals a vulnerable side to them.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.480
Speaker 1: But you see, God is in control.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:32.990
Speaker 1: And having the accountability that there is a God above is what many of them are running away from and gives an enticing feel.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:48.590
Speaker 1: So the next thing that makes it interesting is that it gives a search for healing and answers the question, or seemingly answers the question, can I overcome my pain?

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:55.470
Speaker 1: So New Age spirituality offers very specific ideas about healing, which can be attractive to people who are hurting.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:10.180
Speaker 1: It teaches emotional pain and where it's stored up in the body as negative energy or trauma, fear, anger, past experiences that have created energetic blockages that need to be released.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:24.050
Speaker 1: You know, I spoke to somebody recently who is having this idea closely, and one of the things that made New Age attractive to him is because there are different practice of meditation.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:24.530
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:28.170
Speaker 1: Where some of these things are addressed in a certain form.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.690
Speaker 1: You can call your little self and tell him it was not your fault.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.330
Speaker 1: You know, it makes it really attractive, actually.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.050
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:45.570
Speaker 2: And it reminds me of a popular movie, the animation Avatar, and how some of these practices were actually littered across this movie.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.210
Speaker 2: And many of us watched it as kids.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.810
Speaker 2: I watch it quite young.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:50.210
Speaker 2: Right.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:56.210
Speaker 2: And you see things like, oh, the avatar can't enter into the Avatar state.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.530
Speaker 2: And so because of that, it needs to release starting flows.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:00.850
Speaker 1: Wow.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.690
Speaker 2: There's a chakra that was blocked.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.570
Speaker 2: Things like that, they're right there in the movie.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:11.330
Speaker 2: And now when you are exposed to such a movie and they come in contact with this, it really sounds real.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:11.850
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.250
Speaker 2: And attractive to you that, for example, there is.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.530
Speaker 2: So there is a practice called cord cutting.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:25.870
Speaker 2: And what it basically means is that it's a ritual intended to symbolically or spiritually sever a connection with an unhealthy emotional or spiritual attachment to a person.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:38.910
Speaker 2: So imagine you had a bad breakup and then you discover you are struggling to overcome this breakup and discover that there's actually a practice, a spiritual practice, where in the spirit realm you can cut the cord you have with this person.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.550
Speaker 2: If you are actually not a practicing Christian.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.940
Speaker 2: Again, it sounds attractive.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:44.740
Speaker 1: It sounds attractive.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:45.900
Speaker 1: Sounds attractive because you just.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.700
Speaker 1: You can just smash a button and it's gone.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.260
Speaker 1: And it all seemingly so.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:50.980
Speaker 2: Seemingly so.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:51.460
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:03:02.460
Speaker 1: But you see, one of the beauty of our Christian faith is that the scripture does not merely introduces us to practices of healing, but a God who identifies with our pain.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:11.870
Speaker 1: I think, you know, I went through some crazy stuff this year, and because there were some experiences around that reminded me of some pain.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.910
Speaker 1: But I found the reality of these two scriptures.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:23.710
Speaker 1: Number one, where the Bible says, in Hebrews 4, 15, 16, it says, for we do not have a high priest who is not.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:27.310
Speaker 1: This is an NIV who is not able to empathize with our weaknesses.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:33.950
Speaker 1: But we have one who has been tempted in every way, just as we are, yet he did not sin.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:43.770
Speaker 1: He says, let us then approach God's throne of grace with confidence so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:59.810
Speaker 1: And the second one, I think is in Second Corinthians, chapter 1, from verse 3 to 5, where it says, blessed be the Father of our Lord Jesus, the God of all comforts, who comforts us through all our tribulations, so that we also comfort.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:00.770
Speaker 1: Yes, comfort others.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:01.450
Speaker 2: Comfort others.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.410
Speaker 1: That's powerful.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.890
Speaker 1: A God who is in touch with your need.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.970
Speaker 1: And that's where true healing comes from.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:07.810
Speaker 2: Exactly.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:18.490
Speaker 2: And I think that there is no religion in the world that can claim to understand human suffering and human being like Christianity.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.170
Speaker 2: We don't have a God that stood over there and said, oh, I created you.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.530
Speaker 2: You're going to feel xyz, so just do xyz.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.730
Speaker 2: We have a God who stepped into the pain himself.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:37.690
Speaker 2: You know, I was talking to someone and this person was close to leaving the faith and she was talking about some struggles that she was having and everything.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:43.130
Speaker 2: And I said, do you know, it is interesting that Jesus can actually say, I understand.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.010
Speaker 2: And he's not trying to sound nice.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.970
Speaker 2: He was actually bruised.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.250
Speaker 2: He felt.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:56.570
Speaker 2: If you say that you felt the evil that humans can do, tell that to Jesus.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:57.450
Speaker 1: He felt.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:58.890
Speaker 2: He felt it up to death.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:04:59.530
Speaker 1: Up to death.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:00.090
Speaker 2: Up to death.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.190
Speaker 1: A gruesome death.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:07.590
Speaker 1: It's as though his life was a picture of all the pain and relatability that we could feel.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.470
Speaker 1: And that's the beauty of the Christian faith.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:21.910
Speaker 1: And I've even realized, sorry, this will be the last point I'll make on this, but I've realized that many of the people who embrace this new age spirituality ideas, some of them don't necessarily heal.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.990
Speaker 1: Those are coping mechanisms and escapes.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.460
Speaker 1: Yes, because you find out that there is.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:40.820
Speaker 1: You believe you've healed, but you are projecting in some other ways, maybe through a life without accountability and you don't realize that is a direct consequence.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.980
Speaker 1: But because you are being taught that your Actions are free.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.780
Speaker 1: Every emotion you feel it.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:46.980
Speaker 1: Do you understand what I'm saying?

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.580
Speaker 2: I mean, theology is tight.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:51.940
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:59.680
Speaker 2: I think the final thing that we should talk on is the fact that a lot of people are searching for something beyond the physical world.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:10.240
Speaker 2: And especially if you've already rejected Christianity, then the next available alternative is to go after these practices.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.480
Speaker 2: And I found this quite interesting that there was a.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.520
Speaker 2: There's a popular.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.890
Speaker 2: This guy used to play for a major Nigerian musician from back in the day.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:25.890
Speaker 2: And he said that he learned how to play the instrument by extra projection.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:26.450
Speaker 1: Wow.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:30.210
Speaker 2: And so this was a post on social media, and now he's a Christian.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.890
Speaker 2: He was warning against the practices.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:38.810
Speaker 2: But one thing I found interesting is that under the comments of that post were people asking, how can I do it?

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:39.330
Speaker 2: As well.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:47.730
Speaker 2: People want something, they just want something, not even knowing the consequences, the consequence of it.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.580
Speaker 1: And we are going to get there.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.020
Speaker 1: Yeah, we're going to get there.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:07:03.819
Speaker 1: Just to add one final point, and this can be tempting to people, especially in a world of mental breakdown and isolation, the New Age, I think this is even one of the first things that makes it attractive.

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:08.940
Speaker 1: They have a strong community, and people are always looking to answer the question, where do I belong?

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.060
Speaker 1: And someone can join a yoga group.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.060
Speaker 1: It looks simple.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:16.750
Speaker 1: Join a meditation group, join these, join a hiking group, a travel community.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:20.590
Speaker 1: And they will say things like, we are spiritual, but we are irreligious.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:21.710
Speaker 1: That's how it starts.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:24.750
Speaker 2: Now, imagine such a person suffers church hurt.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:25.390
Speaker 2: Yes,.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:28.990
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:36.550
Speaker 1: And we are saying all of this so that you can understand and diagnose what the problem is, not the symptom.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:44.600
Speaker 1: Some people assume the symptom is the problem, but the symptom, a rebellion, sometimes is from a deeper problem.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:58.240
Speaker 1: And if you can address that, someone who has been hurt, for example, and is now projecting by accepting his ideologies, if you understand and you can showcase the love of God to them directly, you will go a long way in restoring them.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.040
Speaker 1: Okay, so we're just going to run over some of their core theological claims.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:12.720
Speaker 1: Even though some of these, like Pastor Ayuki said earlier, there are several beliefs that make up the New Age spirituality movement.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.160
Speaker 1: So these are just some things that show up as recurring themes all across.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.120
Speaker 1: Yes, all across.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:19.760
Speaker 2: So.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.120
Speaker 2: So one of it is pantheism.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.320
Speaker 2: Now let me explain what pantheism is.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:34.409
Speaker 2: Pantheism is the belief that all reality is one divine substance or consciousness.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:39.049
Speaker 2: So God is not separate from the creation, and everything created is divine.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:52.010
Speaker 2: Basically, it's not as if God stood outside of creation and created the world, but rather the world is an extension of one soul divine being.

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:54.330
Speaker 2: Now, there are implications to this.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:09:02.320
Speaker 2: It means that there is nothing bad in the world, meaning everything in the world is inherently good.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.760
Speaker 2: Now these are the issues and reasons why it is appealing.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.640
Speaker 2: Number one, it eliminates judgment.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.320
Speaker 2: So if all is divine, then nothing is evil.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.480
Speaker 2: Everything is actually sacred.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.800
Speaker 2: Number two, there's a form of unity and belonging in it.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.960
Speaker 2: So if all is one, then I belong to everything and I am never alone.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.320
Speaker 2: I am a part of cosmic oneness.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:33.200
Speaker 2: So again, what we spoke about earlier, in the fact that they are seeking for meaning.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.000
Speaker 2: So this also makes it appealing.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.920
Speaker 2: The next thing is the fact that it makes you transcendent.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:48.240
Speaker 2: So if I am so, that is why, like what Pdams mentioned earlier, someone can come to you and say, do you know I am God?

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:50.320
Speaker 2: And he genuinely believes it.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.830
Speaker 2: So it comes from this ideology.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:59.910
Speaker 2: Now, Peter, do you want touch on theological issues with pantheism?

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:02.710
Speaker 1: Okay, one.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.790
Speaker 1: First of all is the moral contradiction.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.470
Speaker 1: Do everybody objectively knows.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.030
Speaker 1: Like for example, you see, C.S.

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:13.990
Speaker 1: Lewis in his book Mere Christianity made a simple argument.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.110
Speaker 1: We all subscribe to the moral law.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:17.590
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.540
Speaker 1: Without ever speaking about it.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:23.210
Speaker 1: I have three children and my two first boys.

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:28.050
Speaker 1: More often than not, sometimes when they are playing with a toy, they finally agree.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:29.210
Speaker 1: We will take turns.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:34.010
Speaker 1: All of a sudden one of them doesn't want to take the turn and he says, you agreed?

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:36.570
Speaker 1: What made that not fair?

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.890
Speaker 1: By what standard is that not a good act?

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:41.690
Speaker 1: Who taught him?

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.170
Speaker 1: It means that there is a moral law which also gives.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:53.750
Speaker 1: The presence of a moral lawgiver has established intuitively, instinctively, in every man the knowledge of good and evil.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:11:04.310
Speaker 1: However, this idea of pantheism, it contradicts the moral law, meaning if a system cannot distinguish between good and evil, the system cannot be trusted.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:13.750
Speaker 1: And the existence of real evil suffering that is genuinely wrong is incompatible with the claim that all is divine and good.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:15.920
Speaker 2: It's like self deceit.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:16.760
Speaker 1: It's self deceit.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:17.520
Speaker 1: It's an illusion.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:18.960
Speaker 1: Exactly, it's an illusion.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:26.520
Speaker 1: Another problem, I think I'll just run through it quickly so we can move to the second one is the problem of creation.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.400
Speaker 1: If God and the universe are one, how did the universe come to exist?

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.560
Speaker 1: And pantheism has no answer to this.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:38.800
Speaker 1: The question of why something exists rather than nothing remains unanswered.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:45.210
Speaker 2: Especially because we have scientific evidence for the fact that the universe began to exist.

00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:47.370
Speaker 2: So the universe is not eternal.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:48.410
Speaker 2: So how come?

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.330
Speaker 1: Yes, Another problem is the problem of change and time.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:58.810
Speaker 1: If all is God and God is eternal and unchanging, how can anything change?

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.370
Speaker 1: Yet we know that things change.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.730
Speaker 1: People come, people go, people die.

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.090
Speaker 1: There's a false illusion that you are not.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.170
Speaker 1: This is not your real self.

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.040
Speaker 1: But how can you prove that?

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.840
Speaker 1: Meanwhile, all the belief and substance is behind all that.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:18.040
Speaker 1: You see, I think.

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:19.480
Speaker 1: Let me just touch two more.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.720
Speaker 1: The problem of personal relationship.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.640
Speaker 1: You know, how can you have a personal relationship with an impersonal divine force that is everything.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:34.280
Speaker 1: But there needs to be another person for a genuine relationship to be possible.

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.340
Speaker 1: And human beings desperately need relationships.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.460
Speaker 1: We need to be known and loved by one another.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:46.980
Speaker 1: And pantheism where saying that all is God, everything is divine, energy cannot provide answer.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:51.620
Speaker 2: And it's interesting that these people make statements like the universe is telling me something.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:53.740
Speaker 2: But who is the universe?

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:59.460
Speaker 2: Who is the universe if everything is just one divine existence, who is speaking to?

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:06.110
Speaker 2: I mean, you can't even compare it with the Trinity ideology, because the Trinity lets you know that there's one God but three persons.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.590
Speaker 2: Yes, but in this ideology, everything is just.

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:10.510
Speaker 1: And you said it's impersonal.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:11.110
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.830
Speaker 1: So why come there's relationship?

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:15.710
Speaker 2: How come someone is speaking to someone?

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.390
Speaker 1: So these are things to just help you understand theological claims.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.710
Speaker 1: And of course, it eliminates meaning.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:26.480
Speaker 1: When good and evil are not distinct, it eliminates meaning.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:39.800
Speaker 1: Now, another core theological claim that you know is contradictory is the subject of human divinity, where they say, you are God and you need to awaken your divine nature.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:43.520
Speaker 1: I think we've already built on this conclusively.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:51.680
Speaker 1: The reason why this claim is appealing is because it flatters the human pride, the need to feel superior, the need to have an ego.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:57.590
Speaker 1: It also eliminates accountability, because if you're God, you are not accountable to anyone.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:01.830
Speaker 1: It promises unlimited potential, tells you can be anything.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.870
Speaker 1: This just panders to the narrative in Genesis 3.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:07.910
Speaker 1: Like we already said, it removes guilt and shame.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:09.990
Speaker 1: You are not sinful, you are divine.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.870
Speaker 1: What you've done in your life are just lessons.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:16.790
Speaker 1: This is a key theme that I see among people who practice this.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.750
Speaker 1: It doesn't make you to objectively accept wrong.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.440
Speaker 1: Yes, but it just says everything in your life were lessons.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:33.030
Speaker 1: But neither good nor bad, offers power and control, telling you can create your reality and provides identity and significance, telling you are divine.

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:33.510
Speaker 1: Well,.
